cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  How much of the evidence for climate-driven dengue transmission
  survives the analyst? A multiverse analysis of 221 outbreaks in 33
  countries
message: >-
  If you use this software or its results, please cite it using the
  metadata below. The preprint field will be added once the manuscript
  is posted to medRxiv.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Amna
    family-names: Dilber
    email: amnadilber.bi@gmail.com
    affiliation: Independent researcher
repository-code: "https://github.com/amnadilber/dengue-multiverse"
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.21854302
version: v1.0.0
abstract: >-
  A multiverse analysis of climate-attribution inference in mechanistic
  dengue transmission models. Every usable outbreak in a global
  surveillance compilation is fitted under a full factorial of six
  routine analysis choices - the observation model, the functional form
  of the temperature response, the rainfall lag, the fraction of the
  series fitted, the compartmental structure, and the values at which
  the fixed epidemiological parameters are set - giving 144 combinations
  and 33,152 fits across 221 outbreaks in 33 countries.

  Roughly three-quarters of the variation in whether climate forcing is
  endorsed lies between analyses of the same outbreak rather than between
  outbreaks. Partitioned three ways the largest term is neither: 61% is
  an outbreak-by-analysis interaction, against 16% for the analysis
  itself, so no standardised convention can reach it and a specification
  curve, which displays the main effect, cannot show it.

  Conventional 95% confidence intervals cover the truth 71-78% of the
  time on simulated data whose answer is known, and 42% when the epidemic
  is a sum of two asynchronous ones. Combining eight analyses by Rubin's
  rules restores nominal coverage at 1.5-2.2 times the width. Applying
  the paper's own rule to the real data gives a clear verdict for 137 of
  221 outbreaks, 86% of which support climate forcing.
keywords:
  - dengue
  - multiverse analysis
  - specification curve
  - researcher degrees of freedom
  - compartmental models
  - model selection
  - confidence interval coverage
  - climate and health
  - reproducibility
